WEBVTT

00:05.463 --> 00:07.966
<i>I’m Koichi Haimawari.</i>

00:08.049 --> 00:09.634
<i>My hobby is being a hero.</i>

00:09.718 --> 00:11.469
<i>Currently, I’m a university student.</i>

00:11.553 --> 00:16.307
There will be a test next week that covers
chapter three in our textbook.

00:16.391 --> 00:18.268
Please review that section.

00:18.351 --> 00:20.186
BIRTH OF HEROES AND ITS ROOTS
BIRTH OF VIGILANTES

00:20.270 --> 00:21.104
Class is dismissed.

00:22.397 --> 00:24.065
<i>I’m a student, but…</i>

00:27.485 --> 00:31.031
<i>Lately, I’m struggling with school.</i>

00:31.114 --> 00:32.532
<i>I’m in real trouble.</i>

00:34.034 --> 00:36.244
Hey there. Can I sit here?

00:36.327 --> 00:38.038
Sure.

00:40.457 --> 00:41.708
Thanks.

00:42.333 --> 00:46.379
You’ve been staring at the same page
for a while, but what are you reading?

00:46.463 --> 00:47.547
It’s this.

00:47.630 --> 00:50.467
Oh, it’s the textbook for Hero Sociology.

00:51.050 --> 00:52.802
It’s written by my professor.

00:52.886 --> 00:56.681
You’re studying for a test, huh?
That textbook is difficult to understand,

00:56.765 --> 01:00.560
so you should borrow notes
from the seniors in your club.

01:00.643 --> 01:03.688
I’m not part of any clubs…

01:03.772 --> 01:05.857
I see. You’re a loner.

01:05.940 --> 01:06.900
Yes…

01:06.983 --> 01:08.568
I’m a loner.

01:08.651 --> 01:10.195
Must be tough for you.

01:10.278 --> 01:11.613
Yes, it’s tough.

01:13.281 --> 01:14.199
All right, I got it.

01:15.658 --> 01:17.494
You can count on me.

01:17.577 --> 01:18.620
What?

01:19.287 --> 01:20.288
I’m in the Arano Seminar…

01:20.371 --> 01:22.540
Makoto.

01:22.624 --> 01:24.793
Are you hitting on a younger student?

01:24.876 --> 01:26.461
Yay!

01:26.544 --> 01:27.837
Oh, by the way, I’m…

01:27.921 --> 01:31.633
Koichi Haimawari, a first-year student
in the Department of Sociology.

01:31.716 --> 01:34.844
<i>You’re famous</i>
<i>as "The creeper of the Naruhata ruins".</i>

01:36.179 --> 01:38.014
That rumor hasn’t died down yet?

01:38.598 --> 01:40.391
I was just looking for a guy I can use

01:40.475 --> 01:43.561
who’s also familiar with that area.

01:45.063 --> 01:48.566
Of course, I’ll make it worth your while.

01:48.650 --> 01:51.194
I’ll help with your studies,
and you’ll help me with my errand.

01:51.778 --> 01:53.196
It’ll be a give and take.

03:23.995 --> 03:25.538
PROFESSIONAL

03:25.622 --> 03:27.248
A PERSON WHO DOES SOMETHING
AS A PROFESSION AND EARNS A LIVING

03:27.332 --> 03:28.666
ALSO, A WORK EXPERT OR SPECIALIST

03:28.750 --> 03:30.168
WITH SPECIALIZED KNOWLEDGE
AND SKILLS IN A SPECIFIC FIELD

03:38.843 --> 03:40.011
What? Really?

03:42.305 --> 03:44.140
This? A penthouse?

03:44.224 --> 03:47.936
You’re being scammed
by the real estate agent.

03:48.019 --> 03:49.270
I am, huh?

04:05.578 --> 04:06.955
Please wait one second.

04:07.038 --> 04:08.790
I’m going to clean up my room.

04:09.791 --> 04:12.418
You’re a guy, so I’m sure
you’ve got things to hide.

04:12.502 --> 04:14.837
It’s not like that.

04:18.132 --> 04:21.761
Pop left her things all over the place.

04:22.929 --> 04:24.722
Oh crap, I have to hide my hoodie.

04:25.390 --> 04:29.185
It’ll be trouble if it leaks
that I’m The Crawler.

04:30.019 --> 04:31.646
Can I come in now?

04:31.729 --> 04:34.274
Yeah, in a second!

04:35.900 --> 04:38.069
Oh crap, the beer cans!
I can’t leave these out!

04:39.445 --> 04:40.780
Thanks for waiting!

04:42.073 --> 04:45.576
All right, let’s begin with your studies.

04:45.660 --> 04:48.037
Can I see your course
enrollment form if you have it?

04:48.705 --> 04:51.416
First, let’s categorize the classes
you’re taking this year,

04:52.000 --> 04:56.129
into classes that prioritize
"exams", "reports", or "attendance".

04:56.212 --> 05:00.008
I can get you notes from the lectures
and questions from past exams,

05:00.091 --> 05:03.011
but make sure to show up for the classes
that prioritize attendance.

05:03.094 --> 05:04.762
How reliable.

05:05.388 --> 05:08.349
Now, the issue at hand
is your "Hero Sociology".

05:08.433 --> 05:09.976
I’ll give you my notes later on,

05:10.059 --> 05:14.647
but for now, let’s review the parts
that will be covered in the upcoming test.

05:14.731 --> 05:16.733
-The test is on chapter three, right?
-Yes.

05:17.900 --> 05:21.029
Chapter three
of "Introduction to Hero Sociology".

05:21.779 --> 05:25.199
"The conflict between Vigilantism
and the Hero licensing system."

05:25.950 --> 05:27.785
This is perfect.

05:28.453 --> 05:30.830
<i>First of all, academically speaking,</i>

05:30.913 --> 05:33.499
<i>vigilantism is a security system</i>
<i>that takes place naturally</i>

05:33.583 --> 05:36.335
<i>during periods of social turmoil</i>
<i>and unrest.</i>

05:37.253 --> 05:40.214
<i>Most vigilantes just exist</i>
<i>during the period of transition,</i>

05:40.298 --> 05:44.093
<i>and once society stabilizes,</i>
<i>they either become absorbed or eliminated</i>

05:44.177 --> 05:45.678
<i>by the officially authorized system.</i>

05:47.221 --> 05:49.932
That’s exactly what happened
with the Advent of the Extraordinary.

05:50.016 --> 05:52.852
You covered "Birth of Heroes" in modern
history class in high school, right?

05:52.935 --> 05:54.187
Yes,

05:54.270 --> 05:56.856
I feel like I’ve heard about it,

05:56.939 --> 05:59.150
but I’m not so sure either.

05:59.233 --> 06:01.861
<i>Social disorder erupted by the emergence</i>

06:01.944 --> 06:04.614
<i>of humankind's very first</i>
<i>source of power called "Quirks".</i>

06:04.697 --> 06:07.200
<i>That situation brought about vigilantes</i>

06:07.283 --> 06:11.370
<i>among ordinary citizens</i>
<i>who used their "Quirks" to ensure safety.</i>

06:11.996 --> 06:15.083
<i>Soon after that, legislation regarding</i>
<i>the use of Quirks were put in place</i>

06:15.166 --> 06:19.545
<i>and vigilantes became officially</i>
<i>and legally recognized as "Heroes".</i>

06:19.629 --> 06:22.632
Yes, yes, it’s all coming back to me now.

06:22.715 --> 06:24.842
But that’s the story for the public.

06:24.926 --> 06:25.927
What? That’s not true?

06:26.010 --> 06:28.262
It's time for a pop quiz.

06:28.763 --> 06:33.643
<i>Where was the world’s first</i>
<i>Hero Licensing System enacted?</i>

06:33.726 --> 06:37.063
<i>Oh, it was in America…</i>

06:37.146 --> 06:39.357
<i>Yes, it was in Rhode Island.</i>

06:39.982 --> 06:41.359
<i>Back then,</i>

06:41.442 --> 06:45.363
<i>there were 189 vigilantes</i>
<i>who were eligible for the so-called</i>

06:45.446 --> 06:46.864
<i>"The Rhode Island new state statute".</i>

06:47.448 --> 06:49.367
How many of them do you think

06:49.450 --> 06:51.619
were officially recognized as Heroes?

06:51.702 --> 06:52.578
What?

06:52.662 --> 06:56.082
Oh right, not all of them qualified, huh?

06:56.666 --> 07:00.294
I’m sure some of them didn’t have
the qualities of a Hero.

07:00.378 --> 07:02.964
Half of them?

07:03.047 --> 07:04.257
Tsk, tsk.

07:04.340 --> 07:06.425
The answer is just seven.

07:06.509 --> 07:08.928
Most vigilantes were categorized

07:09.011 --> 07:10.763
as Quirk wielding Villains.

07:10.847 --> 07:14.434
<i>There’s truth to the common belief</i>
<i>that "vigilantes with good intentions</i>

07:14.517 --> 07:18.312
<i>were officially recognized</i>
<i>and became the first Heroes".</i>

07:19.814 --> 07:23.734
<i>But the real objective behind</i>
<i>the Hero Licensing System</i>

07:23.818 --> 07:25.987
<i>was not about "authorizing Heroes",</i>

07:26.070 --> 07:29.449
<i>but more about "defining true Villains".</i>
<i>Or so people say.</i>

07:30.950 --> 07:33.202
<i>Individuals who wield Quirks arbitrarily</i>
<i>are categorized as either</i>

07:33.286 --> 07:35.329
<i>"Heroes" or " Villains",</i>

07:35.413 --> 07:37.957
<i>and the former limit the actions</i>
<i>of the latter.</i>

07:38.040 --> 07:41.586
<i>Basically, it’s part of society’s</i>
<i>"Quirk" control system.</i>

07:42.211 --> 07:44.547
Regarding the "control aspect", it's still

07:44.630 --> 07:48.134
a subject of debate as to whether it
violates the citizens’ right to bear arms

07:48.217 --> 07:50.052
and right of self-determination.

07:51.429 --> 07:53.473
It's a type of "Who’s the one
watching over Big Brother",

07:53.556 --> 07:56.517
that’s rooted in distrust towards
the government.

07:57.268 --> 08:00.104
The vigilante ideology that first sparked
the Hero Licensing System

08:00.897 --> 08:03.691
is now serving as a justifiable reason
for denying that very system.

08:03.774 --> 08:05.610
Creating a problem,
then trying to be the solution.

08:05.693 --> 08:06.819
I see.

08:06.903 --> 08:08.529
Therefore,

08:09.113 --> 08:10.364
vigilantism,

08:10.448 --> 08:12.366
also known as "personal sense of justice",

08:12.450 --> 08:15.745
is the origin of "public justice",
also known as the Hero Licensing System.

08:15.828 --> 08:18.581
But at the same time, it also opposes
the idea of surveillance.

08:18.664 --> 08:20.500
That’s the overview of chapter three.

08:21.459 --> 08:23.961
If you write that down,
you’ll be able to pass your test.

08:24.045 --> 08:25.963
Review the parts where I bookmarked.

08:26.047 --> 08:28.216
Wow, you’re a lifesaver.

08:28.299 --> 08:32.178
Since you look like you’re relieved,
let me ask one more question.

08:32.261 --> 08:34.263
When the aforementioned
new law came into effect,

08:34.347 --> 08:35.932
what do you think was the decisive factor

08:36.015 --> 08:38.434
that separated Heroes from Villains?

08:39.560 --> 08:40.603
Not sure…

08:40.686 --> 08:43.648
If they obeyed laws?
Or a strong sense of justice?

08:45.233 --> 08:46.609
The answer to that is a secret.

08:47.276 --> 08:50.446
Oh, come on. Stop teasing me and tell me.

08:51.030 --> 08:54.033
Sorry, this question
has nothing to do with your test.

08:54.116 --> 08:56.494
It’s the theme of my independent research.

08:56.577 --> 08:59.664
If you will cooperate with me,
then I’ll tell you the answer later on.

09:01.499 --> 09:03.251
You will cooperate with me, right?

09:03.334 --> 09:04.585
Yes.

09:04.669 --> 09:07.672
Of course, I’ll do whatever I can to help.

09:07.755 --> 09:09.632
But what is it exactly
that you want me to do?

09:10.216 --> 09:11.342
Well…

09:12.093 --> 09:15.012
Field investigation

09:15.096 --> 09:18.266
on the rumored "Vigilantes of Naruhata"!

09:18.349 --> 09:21.936
By looking into "modern vigilantes",
who are like living fossils,

09:22.019 --> 09:25.022
we can gain a new perspective
in our study on the formation process

09:25.106 --> 09:26.899
of the Hero System.

09:27.650 --> 09:30.903
Most of all, they’re captivating subjects.

09:32.238 --> 09:33.614
What’s the matter?

09:33.698 --> 09:34.657
Nothing…

09:35.241 --> 09:36.576
Well,

09:36.659 --> 09:39.662
I’ve heard about their work too.

09:39.745 --> 09:42.582
They’re contributing a lot
to maintain peace in this town.

09:43.124 --> 09:45.793
But wouldn’t it be dangerous
to carelessly approach people

09:45.876 --> 09:48.296
who are fighting against Villains?

09:48.879 --> 09:51.382
That’s why you'll be my bodyguard!

09:51.465 --> 09:53.759
What? Me?

09:56.387 --> 09:59.640
Then I’ll see you in front of the station
at ten o’clock on Saturday.

10:02.268 --> 10:04.478
She was kind of pushy…

10:04.562 --> 10:06.439
I was kind of steamrolled
into helping her out,

10:06.522 --> 10:08.816
but I’ll have to hide the truth somehow…

10:08.899 --> 10:11.569
Next Saturday, huh…

10:11.652 --> 10:13.279
Why are you grinning?

10:13.904 --> 10:16.115
Pop! You were here?

10:33.007 --> 10:33.883
Hey.

10:35.051 --> 10:36.093
I’m home.

10:36.761 --> 10:38.804
Long time no see, Mr. Police Inspector.

10:39.513 --> 10:41.015
You’re working hard.

10:41.974 --> 10:43.684
You’re working on your thesis?

10:44.935 --> 10:46.646
Yeah, I’m gathering
all my materials together.

10:47.396 --> 10:48.314
By the way, Brother,

10:48.397 --> 10:52.026
you investigate
Quirk-related crimes, right?

10:52.109 --> 10:54.779
Is there any information you can share
about Heroes and Villains?

10:54.862 --> 10:57.073
I can’t leak any information
about our investigations.

10:57.698 --> 11:00.951
Then can you at least hook me up
with a famous Hero for me to interview or…

11:01.035 --> 11:03.621
Don’t you have any connections
with All Might?

11:05.039 --> 11:07.500
Sorry, sorry.

11:08.000 --> 11:08.959
Makoto,

11:09.043 --> 11:12.254
listen to me. Heroes are one thing,

11:12.338 --> 11:15.174
but don’t go around approaching Villains
out of curiosity.

11:15.800 --> 11:19.470
Their existence is always connected
to some sort of a crime.

11:19.553 --> 11:23.641
The politically correct thing to say is,
"Hate the sin but not the sinner," no?

11:23.724 --> 11:25.851
"Heroes and Villains
are two sides of the same coin."

11:25.935 --> 11:28.270
"What's lawful or unlawful
depends on each situation."

11:28.896 --> 11:31.232
That’s my personal stance.

11:32.483 --> 11:35.027
I don’t plan on arguing with you
about this.

11:35.111 --> 11:36.821
I know I can’t win against you with words.

11:37.405 --> 11:41.534
But no matter what your reasoning is,
there are many people who think,

11:41.617 --> 11:44.412
"There’s no problem as long as

11:44.495 --> 11:46.580
I don’t cross the line,"
but end up falling onto the other side.

11:46.664 --> 11:49.625
It goes for both victims and perpetrators.

11:50.543 --> 11:52.795
Thank you for the warning. Basically,

11:53.379 --> 11:54.755
I have to make sure not to get caught.

11:56.215 --> 11:58.050
That’s not what I meant.

11:58.134 --> 12:00.761
I’m telling you not to throw yourself
into dangerous situations.

12:01.470 --> 12:02.972
Who does that woman think she is?

12:03.055 --> 12:05.724
She barged in here
as if she owned this place.

12:05.808 --> 12:09.019
She has no idea how to maintain a proper
distance or where to draw the line.

12:09.103 --> 12:11.397
Isn’t she a little too shameless?

12:11.480 --> 12:13.899
-Yes, yes.
-Why do I have to be the one to hide?

12:13.983 --> 12:15.985
<i>This is unbelievable!</i>

12:23.826 --> 12:25.119
First,

12:25.202 --> 12:28.164
I'll get closer to the opponent
by mixing in some feints.

12:28.247 --> 12:31.041
Then I'll stretch my leg sideways
and hook my opponent.

12:31.125 --> 12:34.295
If the opponent dodges that
and chases me from behind,

12:34.879 --> 12:37.798
I'll stop abruptly
and do a reverse leg sweep.

12:38.382 --> 12:40.509
If the opponent gets cautious
and blocks my way,

12:40.593 --> 12:42.928
I'll escape by going backwards.

12:43.554 --> 12:45.055
I see.

12:45.139 --> 12:47.892
<i>It's hard to read your next move</i>
<i>when you're using Slide and Glide.</i>

12:48.476 --> 12:51.020
<i>If you add the leg sweep</i>
<i>to your attack, it will be</i>

12:51.103 --> 12:53.105
<i>even more difficult</i>
<i>for your opponent to advance.</i>

12:53.898 --> 12:55.816
<i>It's an easier transition</i>
<i>for an escape too.</i>

12:57.318 --> 12:58.819
You came up with a good move, Koichi.

12:59.737 --> 13:01.197
What the hell?

13:01.280 --> 13:03.741
That's so weird and annoying.

13:03.824 --> 13:06.285
If it'll annoy the opponent,

13:06.368 --> 13:08.329
it'll be even more effective.

13:09.788 --> 13:12.458
She's been in a bad mood lately.

13:13.042 --> 13:14.168
The thing is…

13:16.962 --> 13:19.673
What? Field investigation on vigilantes?

13:19.757 --> 13:23.177
Yes, I got roped into helping
a senior at my university

13:23.260 --> 13:24.762
with her research project.

13:24.845 --> 13:26.764
That's not good.

13:26.847 --> 13:29.725
If by any chance, our identities
are leaked to a third party,

13:30.643 --> 13:33.562
the police and other bad guys
will come chasing after us.

13:33.646 --> 13:35.314
I figured as much…

13:35.397 --> 13:37.274
You must be an idiot.

13:37.942 --> 13:41.862
But there are ways to silence those
who come sniffing around.

13:41.946 --> 13:45.824
I will make sure she doesn't find out
about us, so please stay quiet.

13:45.908 --> 13:48.118
Fine, I'll leave things
up to you this time,

13:48.202 --> 13:50.829
but you shouldn't mix
your public and private matters together.

13:50.913 --> 13:52.039
Seriously.

13:52.122 --> 13:56.126
In this case,
what's "public" and what's "private"?

13:58.295 --> 13:59.713
Do as you please.

14:00.548 --> 14:04.718
EAST NARUHATA STATION

14:07.680 --> 14:08.889
Hey.

14:09.723 --> 14:11.976
Koichi-kun, thanks for coming.

14:15.354 --> 14:18.566
Why are you spaced out?
Does my outfit look weird?

14:18.649 --> 14:21.610
No, it really suits you. It's lovely.

14:22.778 --> 14:24.572
That outfit doesn't even look good on her.

14:24.655 --> 14:27.700
Let's get started. I searched
for information on social media

14:27.783 --> 14:31.579
and bookmarked a few spots
where "they" tend to appear.

14:32.162 --> 14:34.790
-Let's start by collecting data there.
-Okay.

14:36.208 --> 14:39.670
But do you think
we'll actually get to see them?

14:39.753 --> 14:42.673
Well, it'll be great if we can
meet them in person,

14:42.756 --> 14:44.383
but what I really want to know

14:44.466 --> 14:46.677
is the public's perception of them.

14:46.760 --> 14:47.636
Perception?

14:47.720 --> 14:50.055
I kind of mentioned it the other day too.

14:50.139 --> 14:51.932
About the element that separated

14:52.016 --> 14:54.393
Heroes and Villains when
the Pro Hero System was first established.

14:54.476 --> 14:57.646
Oh, right. You said you'll tell me
the answer next time.

14:57.730 --> 14:58.606
That's right.

14:59.315 --> 15:01.734
It most likely had nothing to do
with "law" or "ethics",

15:01.817 --> 15:05.404
<i>but more about "support from society".</i>
<i>Simply put, it's "popularity".</i>

15:05.487 --> 15:08.324
<i>What? It's decided</i>
<i>over something so superficial?</i>

15:08.407 --> 15:11.368
<i>Quirks come in different forms</i>
<i>and strengths,</i>

15:11.452 --> 15:14.622
<i>so there are limits to binding that power</i>
<i>with uniform rules.</i>

15:14.705 --> 15:16.916
<i>Therefore, we have no choice but to trust</i>

15:16.999 --> 15:19.335
<i>that each individual will use</i>
<i>their power in a just way.</i>

15:19.418 --> 15:21.378
<i>Modern hero rankings</i>

15:21.462 --> 15:23.964
<i>also have a strong</i>
<i>popularity contest aspect to it, right?</i>

15:24.048 --> 15:26.258
<i>This was even more evident</i>

15:26.342 --> 15:28.719
<i>in times when legislation</i>
<i>was not fully developed.</i>

15:28.802 --> 15:30.846
<i>What I want to find out with this research</i>

15:30.930 --> 15:34.266
<i>is how the "Vigilantes of Naruhata", who</i>
<i>could be called "Old-fashioned Heroes",</i>

15:34.350 --> 15:37.478
<i>are being received by local residents?</i>

15:38.437 --> 15:40.856
That information will most likely
touch upon the core of

15:40.940 --> 15:43.651
the modern Hero System
and super-powered society.

15:43.734 --> 15:46.737
I see. It's very interesting indeed.

15:46.820 --> 15:49.114
I really want to know how "they"

15:49.198 --> 15:50.783
are perceived by the public.

15:50.866 --> 15:52.952
Then, let's start interviewing people.

15:53.035 --> 15:54.244
You're in charge of filming.

15:57.873 --> 15:58.999
Vigilante?

15:59.083 --> 16:01.919
Oh, you mean the unlicensed Heroes?

16:02.002 --> 16:03.963
You're basically talking about
the Cruller?

16:04.046 --> 16:06.966
Well, Cruller is really…

16:07.049 --> 16:08.342
Yes, Cruller is…

16:09.009 --> 16:12.596
Pop☆Step was originally
an idol who's famous online,

16:12.680 --> 16:15.891
but lately, she's been working hard
patrolling the town.

16:15.975 --> 16:17.810
We want her to be careful and stay safe,

16:17.893 --> 16:20.813
but as her fans, we're really happy
when we get to see her around town.

16:20.896 --> 16:24.400
She's such a good girl.
We're rooting for her.

16:24.483 --> 16:26.360
Fist Geezer…

16:26.443 --> 16:28.153
He's super insane.

16:29.113 --> 16:30.948
The Cruller is a little creepy.

16:31.031 --> 16:33.242
He's definitely trying to sneak a peek
up girls' skirts.

16:33.325 --> 16:37.788
When I dropped my wallet,
Cruller picked it up for me.

16:37.871 --> 16:41.000
He's such a good kid,
even though he looks a little, you know…

16:42.209 --> 16:43.627
The Cruller?

16:43.711 --> 16:47.756
A shady guy like that can contribute a lot
more to society and still only be average.

16:48.340 --> 16:49.758
Pop-chan is so cute!

16:49.842 --> 16:51.635
She's got a nice butt!

16:52.344 --> 16:54.847
That girl's name is Pop-chan?

16:54.930 --> 16:56.056
She's got a nice butt.

16:56.640 --> 16:57.516
I think it's about time

16:57.599 --> 17:00.310
for her to move beyond
the "I tried singing this song" level.

17:00.394 --> 17:03.313
After all, what is expected of idols
is the "drama of growth".

17:03.397 --> 17:05.566
Relying on excessive exposure is not good.

17:05.649 --> 17:06.775
First of all, idols are about…

17:07.526 --> 17:11.321
There's a suspicious looking old man
wandering around… So scary.

17:12.072 --> 17:14.324
Fighting Villains with bare hands?

17:14.408 --> 17:17.286
It's impossible. It's just a tall tale!

17:17.870 --> 17:19.955
I heard that my senior's friend's friend

17:20.039 --> 17:23.042
was bludgeoned to death by Fist Geezer.

17:23.125 --> 17:24.501
I'm totally serious.

17:25.252 --> 17:28.213
Popular comments regarding Pop☆Step were,

17:28.297 --> 17:30.674
"Butt" and "Cute".

17:30.758 --> 17:32.342
As for Fist Geezer,

17:33.010 --> 17:35.429
"Don't know him", "Scary", and "Insane".

17:35.512 --> 17:38.724
Cruller was "Creepy",
"Helpful but creepy",

17:38.807 --> 17:41.727
"Seems like a nice guy, but creepy",
"Weird and creepy".

17:41.810 --> 17:43.937
That's the overall review.

17:44.021 --> 17:46.065
A lot of people said "creepy", huh?

17:46.148 --> 17:47.024
FIST GEEZER - SCARY

17:47.107 --> 17:48.108
CRULLER - CREEPY

17:50.194 --> 17:52.071
-My laptop!
-A thief!

17:52.154 --> 17:54.698
It's the Soccer Burglar!

17:55.365 --> 17:57.910
He passes through the crowd
as he dribbles his ball!

17:58.619 --> 18:02.039
He's the <i>fantasista</i> of the thievery world!

18:02.122 --> 18:05.125
But all the dribbling is meaningless.

18:05.209 --> 18:07.628
We should mention something
about his penguin look too.

18:07.711 --> 18:10.255
<i>This thief is Emperor Yotsuura.</i>

18:10.339 --> 18:12.091
<i>His Quirk is Soccer.</i>

18:12.174 --> 18:13.842
<i>Is it really a Quirk?</i>

18:13.926 --> 18:15.177
Stop right there!

18:15.260 --> 18:16.553
Please stay right here.

18:16.637 --> 18:19.056
Stop right there!

18:23.143 --> 18:24.853
Why did you change?

18:25.604 --> 18:27.022
Pop.

18:27.106 --> 18:28.232
This is good news for us.

18:29.066 --> 18:32.402
Now that her laptop is stolen,
that woman will give up and go home.

18:32.486 --> 18:35.489
We can't turn a blind eye to this. Plus,

18:36.156 --> 18:39.076
I have to keep The Crawler's identity
a secret no matter what,

18:39.159 --> 18:41.036
but I also want to show off a little too.

18:42.996 --> 18:46.125
Why are you trying to act cool?

18:56.009 --> 18:58.846
I am the man who has caught up
to the thief! The Crawler!

18:59.763 --> 19:00.889
Take this!

19:00.973 --> 19:02.975
My new move, the High-Speed Double-Sweep!

19:04.434 --> 19:06.145
He dodged while dribbling the ball?

19:07.062 --> 19:09.106
Fine, then I'll create
a distance between us…

19:09.982 --> 19:11.316
EMPEROR SHOT

19:22.452 --> 19:23.537
You better watch out!

19:27.583 --> 19:30.711
-Hey!
-You idiot!

19:30.794 --> 19:32.296
Hey, you guys!

19:32.379 --> 19:33.505
Violence is bad.

19:33.589 --> 19:36.216
-Don't hurt him too much.
-Are you serious?

19:39.344 --> 19:41.430
Excuse me. I'm the victim.

19:41.513 --> 19:43.015
Please let me through.

19:44.183 --> 19:47.394
-Are you all right?
-Well…

19:47.477 --> 19:49.730
I fell when I tried
to retrieve your laptop.

19:50.314 --> 19:51.982
You retrieved it for me?

19:52.065 --> 19:53.483
Well, um…

19:54.151 --> 19:56.862
the Vigilante that you're
currently researching

19:56.945 --> 19:58.864
came over to help,

19:58.947 --> 20:01.074
and he was very reliable.

20:01.158 --> 20:04.870
No, the Cruller wasn't all that helpful.

20:08.665 --> 20:10.918
-Bye!
-Bye, miss!

20:11.001 --> 20:13.128
Thanks for your cooperation.

20:13.212 --> 20:15.255
Koichi-kun, thank you
for helping me out today.

20:15.339 --> 20:17.382
I was able to gather
lots of materials today.

20:17.466 --> 20:18.383
I'm glad.

20:18.467 --> 20:22.596
I wish I could've met
the rumored Vigilante too,

20:22.679 --> 20:24.806
but maybe next time.

20:24.890 --> 20:26.016
She plans on continuing?

20:26.099 --> 20:27.809
Oh, by the way,

20:27.893 --> 20:30.604
I have one last question
I want to ask you, Koichi-kun.

20:35.859 --> 20:36.818
Yes?

20:39.154 --> 20:42.574
You're the Cruller, aren't you?

20:43.367 --> 20:46.286
<i>Makoto Tsukauchi. Her Quirk is Polygraph.</i>

20:46.370 --> 20:49.331
<i>This Quirk is activated with</i>
<i>direct physical contact with the target.</i>

20:49.414 --> 20:53.043
<i>It allows the user to judge</i>
<i>"the authenticity of the target's words".</i>

20:54.461 --> 20:55.462
No, I'm not.

20:55.545 --> 20:56.505
JUDGMENT: TRUTH

20:57.339 --> 20:59.508
Oh, I'm sorry.

21:03.929 --> 21:06.014
Are you having trouble with your thesis?

21:06.723 --> 21:09.226
Kind of. I can make something up

21:09.309 --> 21:11.478
with the materials I've gathered so far.

21:11.561 --> 21:13.230
You can't just "make something up".

21:13.897 --> 21:16.275
You're right. I need something
more substantial.

21:17.192 --> 21:19.778
I thought I had a really good lead.

21:20.862 --> 21:22.739
Am I losing my touch?

21:24.950 --> 21:26.618
The public opinion regarding the Cruller.

21:27.286 --> 21:29.663
"Get your act together",
"Don't get too carried away",

21:29.746 --> 21:32.332
"He doesn't take things seriously",
"Needs to try harder".

21:32.916 --> 21:35.210
Wow, these are really harsh.

21:35.294 --> 21:37.879
Pop☆Step. "Butt", "Butt",

21:37.963 --> 21:39.756
"Bad singer", "She only has her butt",

21:39.840 --> 21:41.383
"Bare your ass a little more".

21:42.676 --> 21:44.553
They don't get it at all.

21:44.636 --> 21:49.141
First of all, I have not officially
approved of the name Cruller!

21:49.224 --> 21:50.976
I'm The Crawler!

21:51.059 --> 21:52.769
Not the Cruller!

21:52.853 --> 21:55.355
What's the point of yelling at me
about it!

23:25.987 --> 23:27.405
CAPTAIN CELEBRITY
STARTING HIS ACTIVITIES IN TOKYO

23:27.489 --> 23:29.783
<i>A man is coming from America</i>
<i>to visit Japan.</i>

23:29.866 --> 23:32.119
<i>His name is Captain Celebrity.</i>

23:32.202 --> 23:36.331
<i>He's kind of frivolous, and I can't</i>
<i>get myself to like him that much.</i>

23:36.414 --> 23:40.293
<i>Next episode is, "Major".</i>
<i>All Might is the best, after all.</i>

23:40.377 --> 23:41.545
Subtitle translation by: Lili Morita
